## A B S T R A C T
Patulin (PAT) is an unsaturated lactone mycotoxin primarily produced by Penicillium expansum and Aspergillus clavatus. Given the potential health risks and economic losses associated with PAT, the rapid detection of PAT using fluorescent aptasensors is of significant importance in evaluating food safety. However, it easily increases the cost and complexity caused by signal labeling. We combined TCPP/BDC- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}}$ mixed ligands functionalized Zr metal–organic frameworks $(\mathbf{Zr{-}M O F_{\mathrm{{mix}}}})$ and terminated three-stranded DNA gates (ttsDNA gates) to fabricate a label-free fluorescent aptasensor for PAT detection. The ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\mathrm{-}}{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}$ system was synthesized via a one-pot strategy and could be used to address the problem of pore size limitation and increase the loading amounts of dyes. TtsDNA gate was integrated into the ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\mathrm{-}}{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}$ system to control the release of dyes, exhibiting a high signal-tobackground ratio. The single-stranded aptamer region in ttsDNA gate situated away from the surface of the Zr$\mathbf{MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}},$ resulting in a natural release of dyes in the absence of PAT. While binding to PAT resulted in targetinduced conformational changes that helped form the hairpin structure of the aptamer. This structure hin­ dered the release of dyes from the pores of $\mathbf{Zr\mathrm{-}M O F_{\mathrm{mix}}},$ thus reducing the fluorescence signals intensity. The stimuli-responsive DNA-gated material provides a platform for PAT analysis under conditions of a low limit of detection $(0.871~\mathrm{pg/mL})$ . Furthermore, the excellent specificity and anti-interference of the fluorescent apta­ sensor make the system suitable for the analysis of apple juice samples. This label-free strategy is cheaper and simper compared with labeled detection, especially for the development of multi-target-detection.
## 1. Introduction
Fungi can readily grow in the pre- and post-harvest stages of fruits, resulting in the accumulation of mycotoxins (Xing et al., 2022). Patulin (PAT) is a dominant mycotoxin commonly found in fruit and fruit-derived products. It negatively affects human health and causes great economic losses (Lopez-Fernandez et al., 2022; Yan et al., 2021). For example, PAT contamination is found in 107 of 214 analyzed sam­ ples with an incidence of $50\%$ , including apple, grapes, pineapple, pear, tomato, and apple juice (Gab-Allah et al., 2022; Ngea et al., 2020; Sohrabi et al., 2021). According to the United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA), the adverse effects on human health can be avoided, and food safety can be ensured if the PAT level is maintained at $50\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . Thus, advanced detection technologies capable of rapidly and sensitively quantifying PAT concentration help keep PAT levels within a safe limit.
High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) is a gold standard in PAT detection. This technique can be coupled with an ultraviolet (UV), fluorescence, or mass spectrometry (MS) detectors (Rosa da Silva et al., 2022). Though these HPLC-based methods can be used for the accurate identification and precise quantification of PAT, they are expensive and time-consuming methods and thus cannot be used to determine food safety on a large scale (Kalinke et al., 2022; Majdinasab et al., 2020; Zhu et al., 2020).
To develop low-cost, simple, and portable detection systems, many approaches have been reported previously for the detection of PAT via electrochemical or fluorescent assays (Hou et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2022b; Yan et al., 2022). For example, Huang et al. developed a molecularly imprinted electrochemical sensor for PAT detection, where the combination of molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP), platinum nanoparticle (PtNPs), thionine, and nitrogen-doped graphene (NGE) is applied to obtain an enhanced sensitivity (Huang et al., 2019). The sensitivity of MIP-based electrochemical sensor has also been improved by electropolymerizing MIP on nitrogen doped graphene quantum dots (N-GQDs) and AuNPs-functionalized Cu-metal organic framework (Au@Cu-MOF) (Hatamluyi et al., 2020). In 2021, He and Dong report an aptamer-based electrochemical method by combining DNA walking machine, gold-platinum core-shell nanorods/iron-based metal-organic frameworks/polyethyleneimine-reduced graphene oxide composites (Pt@AuNRs/Fe-MOFs/PEI-rGO), and $z\mathrm{r}$ -based metal-organic frameworks-labeled oligonucleotides load with methylene blue (MB@Zr-MOFs-cDNA) for PAT detection (He and Dong, 2021). This provides a high sensitivity and specificity. However, excessive nano­ material use, necessary electrode pretreatment, and complicated modi­ fication steps often limit the practical applications of electrochemical methods. Fluorescent assays have attracted much attention in the PAT detection. In particular, a switchable fluorescence sensor toward PAT is developed via –COOH functionalized multiwall carbon nanotubes (CA-MWCNTs) quenched aptamer-tagged carboxyfluorescein (Khan et al., 2020). In addition, Ahmadi et al. and Wu et al. both develop fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) strategies for PAT detection (Ahmadi et al., 2019; Wu et al., 2018). The use of exonuclease and DNA duplex structure improves the sensitivity of the fluorescent aptasensors.
Fluorescent aptasensors can be effectively used for PAT detection owing to two core factors: (1) The in vitro screening of PAT aptamers externally added is realized following a fluorescence-based method (Wu et al., 2016). This simplifies the development of fluorescent aptasensors for PAT detection; (2) Signal transduction can be observed in homoge­ neous solutions when fluorescent aptasensors are used. This helps in obtaining accurate results even if there is a batch difference (Adeel et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021a). Typically, signal molecules are labeled at one end of DNA oligonucleotides to provide signal sources for the con­ struction of fluorescent aptasensors, including organic dyes and lumi­ nescent materials (Shen et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022a). This makes the method cost-prohibitive, and a complicated labeling step is required to fabricate the signal molecules (Suo et al., 2020). These limitations are not conducive to the widespread use of fluorescent aptasensors. Thus, the development of label-free fluorescent aptasensors that carry numerous signal molecules is urgently needed to enhance this technology.
Stimuli-responsive DNA-gated materials are native label-free nano­ carriers that can be used to achieve the controlled release of guest molecules in the presence of target molecules (Lu et al., 2021). In the past decades, microcapsules, hydrogels, and mesoporous $\mathrm{{siO}_{2}}$ nano­ particles have been integrated with DNA to fabricate stimuli-responsive systems that are widely used in the fields of drug delivery, imaging, and biosensing (Climent and Rurack, 2021; Gu et al., 2021; Kim et al., 2021). However, these systems require the use of a suitable porous material for loading guest molecules and a precise DNA gate for biosensing.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising candidates used to load guest molecules. These consist of metal ions or clusters connected via organic linkers (Chang et al., 2020; Li et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2021a). The MOFs are characterized by high porosity, large surface area, and tunable structures, making them attractive as stimuli-responsive mate­ rials (Zhou et al., 2021). The amount of dye that can be loaded into MOFs is 16-fold higher than the amount that can be loaded into meso­ porous $\mathrm{{SiO}_{2}}$ nanoparticles. The MOFs contain homogenous pores (Cao et al., 2019), and these features make them suitable and attractive candidates for drug delivery, imaging, and biosensing applications. The research group headed by Willner recently developed $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF and MOF-5 systems as drug/dye carriers to realize the controlled release of dyes and drugs for drug delivery and cancer cell imaging (Kahn et al., 2017; Zhang et al., 2021b). The high loading capacity and tailored DNA modification make MOFs effective stimuli-responsive materials that can be used for biosensing (Chen et al., 2017; Wang et al., 2017). Substantial research efforts demonstrate that this system can be used for the detection of various targets, including microRNAs, carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA), and adenosine triphosphate (ATP) (Chang et al., 2019; Chen et al., 2017; Liu et al., 2021b). The high stability of Zr-MOF con­ tributes to the loading of dyes, and the amino groups present in the li­ gands help in the immobilization of DNA (Meng et al., 2016; Tan et al., 2015). However, the entry of these dyes into the MOFs is limited by the pore size of MOFs. Therefore, it is important to fabricate MOFs with both large pore sizes and high stability to achieve effective dye loading.
Many MOF-based aptasensors are based on the target-induced release of the loaded signal molecules, which need a DNA gate to ach­ ieve the quantitative detection of targets. Various research groups have designed duplexed aptamer (DA) gates by hybridizing an aptamercomplementary element. The use of the method could result in the disassociation of the DAs when the target competes with the comple­ mentary strand to bind to the aptamer (Yu et al., 2021). Although sorts of DA gates based on a target-induced release strategy have been used to achieve signal transduction, the complementary strand must partially mask the target binding domain (Munzar et al., 2017; Zhang et al., 2021c). This competition-based transduction often requires tedious optimization to control the balance between the transducing efficiency and the minimized background (Rangel et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2021c). The limitation of the target-induced release strategy caused by the competition-based transduction should be removed. Compared with the target-induced release strategy, a target-induced locking strategy based on ligand-oligonucleotide transduction achieves a conformational shift from an “open” state to a “closed” state, overcoming the limitations encountered in the competition-based transduction. In addition, one DA gate is responsible for dye release in only one pore of the MOFs. This is one of the primary obstacles to achieving a high signal-to-background ratio with aptasensors. Many researchers have focused on the con­ struction of aptasensors. The aptamer target interaction has not been studied in detail to understand the binding mechanism and target binding domain (Bottari et al., 2020). Therefore, it is challenging to integrate aptamers into DNA gates for signal transduction.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
Zirconium(IV) chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ , 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{(NH}_{2}.$ BDC), tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP), 1,6-hexanediamine, iron chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ , and rhodamine 6G (Rh 6G) were purchased from Aladdin Bio-Technology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). $N,N.$ -Dimethylformamide (DMF) and an aqueous solution of glutaraldehyde $(25\%)$ were purchased from ChengDu Chron Chemicals Co., Ltd (ChengDu, China). Apple juice was purchased from Beijing Huiyuan Food and Beverage Co., Ltd (Beijing, China). Ultrapure water was obtained from a ULPHW-II water system. Tris-HCl $(50~\mathrm{mM}$ , $\mathrm{\pH=}$ 7.4; washing buffer) was purchased from Aladdin Bio-Technology Co., Ltd. The binding buffer was prepared in Tris-HCl $\mathrm{50~mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}=7.4)\$ supplemented with $150\ \mathrm{mM\NaCl}$ and $2\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{Mg}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ . The PAT aptamer with a dissociation constant (Kd) of $21.83\pm5.022\mathrm{nM}$ was used for the construction of aptasensors (Wu et al., 2016). All DNA oligonucleotides obtained from Sangon Biotechnology (Shanghai, China) were purified using the HPLC technique (Table S1).
## 2.2. Characterization
Scanning electron microscopes (SEM, ZEISS Gemini 300 and SEM450, Germany and USA, respectively) were used to monitor the sur­ face morphology of the prepared MOFs. The structure of the systems and the distribution of elements in the MOFs and DNA-functionalized MOFs were analyzed using a transmission electron microscope (TEM, FEI Talos F200i). The elemental mapping profiles and line scanning profiles were obtained via energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). The phase composition of the MOFs was analyzed using X-ray diffraction (XRD, Smart Lab) in the range from $5^{\circ}$ to $50^{\circ}$ , with a scan rate of $2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR, Vertex 70, Germany) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific K-Alpha, USA) techniques were used to determine the chemical constituents of the samples. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method (Micromeritics ASAP 2460, America) was used to calculate the specific surface area and pore volume. The zeta-potential of the samples was measured using a dynamic light scatting (DLS) system (ZEN 3600, Malvern Instruments). The nucleic acid concentration was estimated using a microvolume spectrophotometer (mySPEC, Austria). Fluorescence spectral profiles were recorded on an F98 fluorescence spectrometer (China). The HPLC data were collected on an LC-2030 PLUS instrument.
## 2.3. Synthesis of Zr-MOFmix
## 2.4. Preparation of $F e_{3}O_{4}–N H_{2}$
$\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–N H_{2}}$ was used in the surface-based assay to load the DAs and measure the binding affinity between the aptamer and PAT. $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–N H_{2}}$ was synthesized following previously reported protocols (Wang et al., 2006). 1,6-Hexanediamine $_{(6.5~g)}$ , anhydrous sodium acetate $_{(2.0\ g)}$ , and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $_{(1.0~\mathrm{g)}}$ were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethylene glycol by heating the mixture at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a water bath. The black precipitate was collected via magnetic separation, washed with ultrapure water three times, and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.5. $F e_{3}O_{4}$ -based fluorescence assay
## 2.5.1. Synthesis of DAs
Prior to modification, the DAs were prepared by mixing various concentrations of aptamers with complementary strands (CSs) of the same concentration in a volume ratio of 1:1.5. The mixture was heated at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and then cooled at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Subsequently, the mixture was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain the desired DAs.
## 2.5.2. Synthesis of DA-modified $F e_{3}O_{4}$
For DA immobilization, glutaraldehyde $(25\%$ , $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ was added to $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}–N H_{2}}$ $\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}1.0\mathrm{\mg/mL}$ , $1~\mathrm{mL}$ ) in ultrapure water. The mixture was incubated on a $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ shaker for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . A magnet was used to remove the supernatant, and the collected black precipitate was redispersed in $1\mathrm{mL}$ of Tris-HCl buffer. A solution of the DAs $(200~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was added to the black suspension, and the resulting mixture was incubated on a $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ shaker for another $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . DA-modified $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was obtained after magnetic sepa­ ration. The obtained particles were washed with Tris-HCl buffer and resuspended in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of Tris-HCl buffer. PAT $200~\upmu\mathrm{L},$ , $10~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ was added after $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the suspension was magnetically separated. The supernatant was not analyzed until the DA-modified $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles were incubated for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ using a $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ shaker. The fluorescence intensity was analyzed using Origin 2019b software to fit equation $\boldsymbol{\Upsilon}=\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Bmax}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}/$ $(\operatorname{Kd}+\operatorname{X})$ , where $\mathtt{Y}$ is the fluorescence intensity at different concentra­ tions of PAT aptamers, Bmax is the maximum fluorescence intensity, and X represents the concentration of the PAT aptamers.
## 2.6. Molecular docking to predict aptamer-PAT binding interactions
The secondary structure of the PAT aptamer was predicted by the Mfold web server (http://www.unafold.org/mfold/applications/dn a-folding-form.php). Based on the predicted 2D structure of the mini­ mum free energy PAT aptamer, the data in the file in the Vienna output format (dot-bracket notation) was used to construct the 3D structure of the PAT aptamer (http://biophy.hust.edu.cn/new/3dRNA). The 3D structure of PAT was obtained from the ZINC database (https://zinc.doc king.org/). After obtaining the 3D structures of the PAT aptamer and PAT, the molecular docking process was performed using the Auto­ DockTools 1.5.6 package. The default parameters were used during Vina docking to predict the docking result, and the best scoring pose was visualized using PyMOL.
## 2.7. Synthesis of glutaraldehyde-modified MOFs
The dried amine-functionalized MOFs ( $\mathrm{16~mg}$ were dispersed in 16 mL of ultrapure water. Subsequently, $4\mathrm{mL}$ of glutaraldehyde $(25\%)$ was added to the suspension, and the resulting suspension was shaken for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Glutaraldehyde-modified MOFs (GMOFs) were collected after centrifugation $\cdot10\ \mathrm{min}$ at $7000~\mathrm{rpm}$ ) and washed with ultrapure water three times.
## 2.8. Synthesis of ds, ts, and ttsDNA-functionalized MOFs
## 2.8.1. dsDNA-functionalized MOFs
1-CS $(40\upmu\mathrm{L},$ , $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{M}})$ was added to a microtube containing $1\mathrm{mg}$ of GMOFs in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of Tris-HCl buffer (pH 7.4, $20~\mathrm{mM},$ ). The mixture was shaken for $16\mathrm{h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ After centrifugation (2 min at $11000\mathrm{rpm}\mathrm{,}$ , the products were washed with Tris-HCl buffer. The solution was combined with a solution of $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of Rh 6G $(0.864\ \mathrm{mM})$ to load the dye inside the pores. The mixture was left to incubate on a shaker at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Subsequently, the MOFs were mixed with Tris-HCl buffer and hybridized with $6~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of the aptamers $(100~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to yield the dsDNA gate. The solution was centrifuged again and washed twice with the TrisHCl buffer to remove all traces of surface-adsorbed Rh 6G. The dsDNAfunctionalized MOFs were collected and stored in the refrigerator at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.8.2. tsDNA-functionalized MOFs
The synthesis of tsDNA-functionalized MOFs followed the same protocol as that of the dsDNA-functionalized MOFs (modification: $20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 2-CS1 $(100\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and $20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 2-CS2 ${\bf\dot{100}\upmu M})$ were used in place of 40 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 1-CS ( ${\bf\dot{100}\mu M)}$ during synthesis).
## 2.8.3. ttsDNA-functionalized MOFs
The synthesis of ttsDNA-functionalized MOFs followed the same protocol as that of the dsDNA-functionalized MOFs (modification: DNA 1 consisting of $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 2-CS1 $(100~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 2-CS3 $(100~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ were used instead of $40~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of 1-CS $(100~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ ).
## 2.9. Fluorescence-based detection of PAT
For the detection of PAT, $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of DNA-functionalized MOFs $\cdot1\mathrm{mg}/$ mL) was incubated with different concentrations of PAT $(100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ and Tris-HCl $(50~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ After centrifugation, the supernatant was collected to measure the fluorescence of the released dye using a fluorescence spectrophotometer.
In the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -based fluorescence assay, FAM was labeled at the ${}_{3{}^{,}}$ end of the CS. Details of FAM-labeled DNA were listed in Table S1. The fluorescence of FAM was excited at $486\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and the fluorescence emission spectra were recorded from $505~\mathrm{nm}$ to $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ in a $350~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ quartz cuvette.
In the DNA-functionalized MOFs assay, the released Rh 6G was excited at $420\mathrm{nm}$ , and the fluorescence emission spectra were recorded from $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $650\mathrm{nm}$ in a $350~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ quartz cuvette.
## 2.10. Agarose gel electrophoresis (AGE)
A $3\%$ agarose gel was prepared. All DNA gates (ds, ts, and ttsDNA) were prepared in Tris-HCl buffer, and the gates were heated at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ to make the strands dissociate fully. The gates began to hybridize when they were cooled to room temperature and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The size of DNA gates as follows: dsDNA gate (Apt 1-CS: $40\mathrm{bp}{+}15$ bp); tsDNA gate (Apt 2-CS $_{1+2}$ -CS 2: 40 bp $+7$ bp $^{+8}$ bp); ttsDNA gate $(\mathrm{Apt{+}2{\cdot}C S1{+}2{\cdot}C S3{\cdot}40\ b p{+}7\ b p{+}8\ b p})$ .
The AGE was analyzed according to our previous report (Yan et al., 2021). First, $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of DNA sample was mixed with $1~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $6\times$ loading buffer to prepare the loading sample. Then, the AGE was run in TAE buffer $(1\ \times\ ,\ \mathsf{p H\ 8.0})$ as running buffer, and with a $100{\mathrm{~V~}}$ constant voltage at room temperature for $35\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, it was imaged using a Bio-Rad imaging system.
## 2.11. Apple juice sample preparation
We purchased apple juice with $0\%$ protein and fat at a local super­ market to evaluate the application capability of the proposed fluorescent aptasensor in real samples. To accurately detect PAT in apple juice, some measures need to be taken. Apple juice was pretreated according to a previously reported method (Khan et al., 2020). Specifically, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of apple juice containing different PAT concentrations $\mathrm{(10~ng/mL}$ , 50 $\mathrm{ng/mL}$ , $80~\mathrm{ng/mL}$ , and $100~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ ) were mixed with $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethyl ac­ etate solution. After vortex and centrifugation at $5000\times g$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ , the supernatant was collected and dried under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Then, it was dissolved in Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ and filtered with a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ luer syringe filter. Finally, the filtrate was used for PAT analysis.
## 2.12. Statistical analysis
All assays were conducted in triplicate, and the data were analyzed using SPSS Statistics, Excel, and Origin software.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of Zr-MOF and $Z r-M O F_{m i x}$
Fig. 1B–G presents representative SEM images of the different Zr$\mathrm{MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}}s$ . The sizes of the $\mathbf{Zr{\mathrm{-}}M O F_{\mathrm{{mix}}}}$ nanoparticles prepared using 0–15 mg of TCPP were ${<}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ . An obvious change in morphology was observed (Fig. 1G) when $17.5\mathrm{mg}$ of TCPP was used during synthesis. We analyzed the XRD patterns to explore the phase evolution of different $z\mathrm{r}$ - $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}}}$ and to understand the influence of the amount of TCPP on the morphology. As shown in Fig. 1H, all characteristic peaks of Uio- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ (curve a) closely matched the peaks representing the simulated Uio-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . No significant change in the position and nature of the XRD peaks was observed after introducing the TCPP ligand $(7.5\mathrm{-}15.0\mathrm{mg}$ ; curve b to curve e). This indicated TCPP integration did not significantly affect the crystal structure of Uio- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ . However, new peaks appeared in the range of $9.5\substack{-11.5^{\circ}}$ when $17.5\mathrm{mg}$ of TCPP was added (curve f) into the system, indicating excessive TCPP would result in the generation of an impure phase of Uio- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The FT-IR profiles (Fig. 1I) revealed the presence of high-intensity double peaks at $3350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which were ascribed to the amino groups of ideal Uio- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ (Wang et al., 2021). The peak at $662~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the vibration of $z{\mathrm{r}}{-}0$ (Wang et al. 2021). A gradual weakening in the intensity of the peaks corresponding to amino groups and $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ of $\mathrm{Uio}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was observed with an increase in the TCPP content. This could be attributed to the presence of occupied coordination sites of the $\mathbf{\Delta}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster and ${\tt B D C}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . This also suggested the successful integration of TCPP into MOFs. Notably, the intensity of the amino peak decreased significantly when the amount of TCPP reached $17.5\mathrm{mg}$ (purple area in Fig. 1I). This was not favorable for DNA modification. To explore MOFs with high loading capacity, we measured the fluorescence intensity of the super­ natant after the dye was adsorbed on different Zr-MOFs. Fig. 1J presents the changes in the TCPP-dependent fluorescence intensity. The fluo­ rescence intensity corresponding to the supernatant decreased with an increasing TCPP content. The loading amounts of Uio- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and ${\bf{Z r{-}M O F_{\mathrm{{mix}}}}}s$ could be estimated by analyzing the calibration curve generated for the Rh 6G solution (Fig. S1). The loading amounts were calculated to be approximately 31.6, 76.6, 80.0, 85.9, 104.8, and 213.9 $\mathrm{\nmol/mg}$ . It was noted that the loading for $\mathrm{Zr{-}M O F_{\mathrm{mix}}{-}}15$ was more than 3-fold higher than that recorded for Uio- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and 1.50-fold higher than that recorded for the reported MOF systems (Kahn et al., 2017). This was attributed to the formation of defects and larger pores. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ absorption–desorption isotherms were recorded to confirm the struc­ tural changes in Uio- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ after TCPP integration and Rh 6G loading (Fig. S2). The BET surface areas of Uio- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf15}$ were $421~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $371~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, and the corresponding pore vol­ umes were $0.25~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.56~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively (Fig. S2A). The decrease in the BET surface area and increase in pore volume were attributed to the integration of TCPP into the Uio- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ system. Notably, the mesoporous pores in ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf15}$ allowed the entry of Rh 6G (approximately $1.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ long) into the MOFs (Fig. S2B). After Rh 6G loading, the BET surface area and pore volume of ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf15}$ decreased significantly to $180~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.28~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, and the decrease was attributed to the successful loading of Rh 6G into the pores (and not on the surface of ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf{-}}{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf1}{\bf5};$ ; Fig. S2C). Unfortunately, the supernatant turned purple when the amount of TCPP was increased to $17.5\mathrm{mg}$ , and this amount was not suitable for Rh 6G loading (Fig. 1K). Analysis of the XRD patterns revealed the introduction of an excess of TCPP can potentially result in the dissociation of TCPP in $\mathbf{Zr{\mathrm{-}}M O F_{\mathrm{{mix}}}}$ The FT-IR and SEM results revealed ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf15}$ was a good choice for improving the extent of Rh 6G loading.
3.2. Characterization of DNA gates and their applicability in the field of PAT detection
DNA gates function as switches to control the release of dyes from stimuli-responsive materials, and they are associated with the detection performance of the aptasensor. We designed three DNA gates (DNA gate 1: dsDNA gate, DNA gate 2: tsDNA gate, and DNA gate 3: ttsDNA gate) by changing the hybridization domain of CS to obtain a high signal-tobackground ratio. The detailed sequences are shown in Table S1. We performed a simulation analysis using NUPACK software (http://www. nupack.org/partition/new) to verify the successful synthesis of these three DNA gates. As shown in Fig. 2A–C, the low free energy of the DNA gates and the high equilibrium probability of hybridization between the aptamer and the CSs confirmed the structural stability of the DNA gates. The AGE results revealed the migration of the bands following the hy­ bridization of CS and the aptamer (Fig. 2D–F). This further confirmed the formation of the DNA gates. Further experiments must be conducted to verify the minimal band migration observed after 2-CS3 hybridization in the case of DNA gate 3. Therefore, the designed DNA gate 3 was further analyzed by conducting a $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -based fluorescence assay (Fig. S3A). The FAM signal originating from the 2-CS3 oligonucleotide (after the addition of the 2-CS1/Apt duplex) was significantly lower than the signal recorded for the blank control $(\mathbf{P}<0.001\$ ) (Fig. S3B). The results verified the synthesized DNA gate 3 was stable enough to avoid the formation of an unexpected DNA structure.
We measured and calculated the changes in the fluorescence in­ tensity corresponding to the supernatant, following the addition of PAT at different time intervals, to verify the feasibility of the aptasensor and to evaluate the performance of the three DNA gates for controlled Rh 6G release. Fig. 2G–I presents the changes in the fluorescence intensity, and the intensities reached a saturation point at $60\ \mathrm{min}$ . The maximum changes in the fluorescent intensity and signal-to-background $(\Delta\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{F}_{0})$ ratio were recorded for the DNA gate 3-functionalized MOFs (Fig. 2G–I, and Fig. S4). As expected, the unlocking of the pores and the release of Rh 6G from the pores of DNA gate-functionalized MOFs after the addi­ tion of PAT resulted in an increase in the intensities, corresponding to the DNA gate 1-functionalized MOFs and DNA gate 2-functionalized MOFs. On the contrary, a significant decrease in the fluorescence in­ tensities, corresponding to DNA gate 3-functionalized MOFs, was observed following the addition of PAT. This can be potentially attrib­ uted to the locking of pores induced by PAT. Although DNA gate 3-func­ tionalized MOFs are promising candidates for fluorescent aptasensors, the novel detection mechanism must be further explored, including the binding mechanism and binding sites of the aptamers and PAT.
3.3. Characterization of the DNA-functionalized MOFs based on Zr$M O F_{m i x^{-}}15$ and DNA gate 3
XPS was used to evaluate the chemical constituents of the DNAfunctionalized MOFs and to confirm the successful immobilization of DNA 1 (2-CS1 and 2-CS3) onto the surface of ${\bf{Z r}{-}}\mathbf{M}{\bf{O}}\mathrm{{F_{\mathrm{{mix}}}}}$ -15. The $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ , C 1s, N 1s, and O 1s peaks were attributed to ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf{-}}{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf1}5$ (Fig. 3H). The P 2p peak (atom $2.72\%$ at $133.2\mathrm{eV}$ appeared after DNA 1 modification (Yu et al., 2020), confirming DNA 1 was attached to the surface of ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf{-}}{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf1}5$ (Fig. 3I). Moreover, the $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ peaks exhibited a red shift following the attachment of DNA 1 onto ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf15}$ (Fig. 3J). The density of DNA 1 $\operatorname{\rho}(\mathbf{C}_{\mathbf{x}})$ was estimated to be $0.5\mathrm{\nmol/mg}$ using a microvolume spectrophotometer. The density was calculated using the
following equation:
$$
\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{x}}=(\mathbf{C}_{0}-\mathbf{C}_{1})^{\mathrm{{*}}}\mathrm{n}_{0}/(\mathbf{C}_{0}{^{\mathrm{*}}}\mathrm{m})
$$
where ${\bf C}_{0}$ and $\mathsf C_{1}$ represent the DNA concentrations in the supernatant of $\mathbf{Zr{\mathrm{-}}M O F_{m i x}{\mathrm{-}}}15$ before and after DNA 1 modification, respectively; ${\bf n}_{0}$ denotes the amount of substance added to the nucleic acid; and m de­ notes the amount of $\mathbf{Zr{\mathrm{-}}M O F_{m i x}{\mathrm{-}}}15$ .
The FT-IR profiles present the chemical compositions of ttsDNA- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{Z}}{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}$ - $\mathbf{MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}^{-15}}$ . As shown in Fig. 3K, the peak at $3451~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , associated with the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups (Wang et al., 2021), shifted significantly to $3445~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ when DNA 1 was modified onto $\mathrm{Zr{-}M O F_{\mathrm{mix}}{-}}15$ . The orange region in Fig. 3K showed the second derivative of the shifted region. Moreover, the intensity of the peak at $3341~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , belonged to $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups, decreased significantly after DNA 1 modification. Obviously, there were differences between the spectrum of ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf15}$ and others (DNA $1/\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFmix-15, Rh 6G-loaded DNA $1/\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{\mathrm{-MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}}}\mathrm{-}15$ , and ttsDNA-Zr $\mathbf{\dot{\tau}}_{\mathrm{MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}}-15}$ ). Under these conditions, the corresponding peak of amino group shifted significantly. The subsequent modification of Rh 6G and Apt did not produce an obvious effect on the peaks corre­ sponding to ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{r}}{\bf{-}}{\bf{M}}{\bf{O}}{\bf{F}}_{\mathrm{{mix}}}{\bf{-}}{\bf{1}}5$ . The obtained data demonstrate the amino groups play a key role in DNA immobilization.
A change in the zeta potential during stepwise functionalization was observed (Figure 3L). In the beginning, a positive zeta potential was recorded for ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{r}}{\bf{-}}{\bf{M}}{\bf{O}}{\bf{F}}_{\mathrm{{mix}}}{\bf{-}}{\bf{1}}5$ , and this was attributed to the existence of the amino groups. After DNA 1 modification, the zeta potential became negative as DNA carried numerous negatively charged phosphate backbones. The loading of positively charged Rh 6G resulted in a slight increase in the zeta potential. When Apt was hybridized further, the potential declined once again, and this was attributed to the increase in the number of negatively charged phosphate backbones. The changes in the zeta potential proved the successful synthesis of ttsDNA- $.\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}_{\mathrm{mix}}.$ - 15.
Analysis of the EDS-maps and the line scanning profiles proved the elements Zr, C, N, O, and P were primarily distributed on the octahedral structure. The existence of the C element around MOF was attributed to the natural release of Rh 6G (Figure 3L). Analysis of the EDS profiles also revealed the presence of Zr, C, N, O, and P elements in the ttsDNA-Zr$\mathrm{MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}^{-15}}$ complex, suggesting the successful modification of DNA and encapsulation of Rh 6G (Fig. S7). Moreover, the weight percentage of the elements with different modifications demonstrated the change from Uio- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to ttsDNA- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf1}{\bf5}$ . The decrease in the content of the Zr element (from Uio- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathrm{VH}_{2}}$ to $\mathbf{Zr{\mathrm{-}}M O F_{m i x}{\mathrm{-}}}15$ ) indicated the successful synthesis of ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf{-}}{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}_{\mathrm{mix}}{\bf-}{\bf1}5$ , due to the site-occupying effect of TCPP generated when BDC- $\mathrm{\cdotNH_{2}}$ coordinated with the Zr ions. The origin of the P element was attributed to the phosphates in DNA 1 and Apt. All data proved the successful synthesis of ttsDNA-Zr- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}_{\mathrm{mix}}\mathbf{-}15$ .
## 3.4. Binding mechanism for the aptamers and PAT
The molecular docking method was used to understand the binding mechanism and binding sites associated with the aptamers and PAT. Fig. S8 presents the secondary structure of the aptamer with the lowest free energy. A small hairpin structure at the 5’ end of the aptamer was observed. The secondary structure of the aptamer in the dot bracket format was used to obtain the 3-D conformation of the aptamer using RNAcomposer. Based on the principle of scores obtained using 3dRNAscore (the lower, the better), aptamers (Apt-F2 and Apt-F5) of the two 3-D structures with the lowest score were selected for docking with PAT. The docking results showed that the binding energies of Apt-F2 and Apt-F5 with PAT were $-4.22\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ and $-4.2\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ , respectively, indicating that both the systems could effectively bind to PAT. The binding mechanism of Apt-F2 and Apt-F5 with PAT were further analyzed using Autodock. As shown in Fig. 4B and Fig. 4C, PAT was surrounded by Apt-F2 (characterized by the presence of a small hairpin structure) and Apt-F5 (characterized by the presence of a large hairpin structure). This confirmed the formation of unique hairpins when PAT bound to aptamers. Detailed analysis revealed the presence of two binding regions. Hydrogen bonds were formed between aptamers and PAT (Fig. 4D and G). The aptamer residues G6 and C7 of the Apt-F2 unit interacted with the oxygen atom of PAT. In another case, the aptamer residues T20, C21, and C26 of Apt-F5 interacted with the oxygen atom of
PAT. These hydrogen-bond interactions played a significant role in stabilizing the PAT–aptamer complex. It was apparent that the docking result provided five binding sites for the binding of aptamer with PAT. The possible binding mechanism involves the formation of the hairpin structure when PAT binds to the aptamer.
To verify our hypothesis that the binding sites were located in the abovementioned hairpin structures, we designed DAs by hybridizing DNA in these two potential binding regions. We conducted a $\mathbf{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}.$ - based fluorescence assay (Fig. 4A) to determine the binding affinity (Kd) of Apt-CS F2 (Fig. 4E), which was $140.16\pm78.96~\mathrm{nM}.$ For Apt-CS F5 (Fig. 4H), the Kd was $92.08\pm17.65~\mathrm{nM}$ . Given that no induced-fit binding was observed when the binding site was left unhybridized, it can be inferred that each hybridization region consisted of binding sites for PAT recognition.
We mutated one nucleobase from both loops to effectively study the binding sites of the aptamer. The mutations in Apt-CS F2 (G6 of the aptamer was substituted by T6; Fig. 4F) and Apt-CS F5 (T20 of the aptamer was substituted by G20; Fig. 4I) resulted in a decrease in the change in fluorescence (ΔF), indicating the key recognition sites within the aptamer sequence for PAT. Although this data could not be used to identify a preferred site of PAT binding, T20 mutation produced a remarkable effect on PAT binding. The ΔF corresponding to the T20 mutation decreased by 21-fold, whereas $\Delta\mathsf{F}$ corresponding to the G6 mutation decreased by 2-fold. The results are in agreement with the previous Kd analysis. This difference can be potentially attributed to the interaction between PAT and T20, which induced the formation of a large loop that could stabilize the PAT–aptamer complex. The results are consistent with those obtained from molecular interaction analysis (Trinh et al., 2022).
## 3.5. Possible detection mechanism of DNA-functionalized MOFs for PAT detection
Two CSs (2-CS 1 and 2-CS 3) were used to modify the surface of Zr$\mathbf{MOF}_{\mathrm{mix}^{-15}}$ following the glutaraldehyde crosslinking method, as shown in Scheme 1A. After Rh 6G dyes were encapsulated into the MOFs, the two ends of the Apt unit were hybridized with CSs for capping. The natural release of the Rh 6G dyes from the pores of the MOFs in a buffer solution was attributed to the presence of the single-stranded DNA re­ gion situated away from the surface of the MOFs. The formation of the PAT–aptamer complex resulted in the formation of a stable large hairpin loop (Scheme 1C). This structure blocked the pores loaded with Rh 6G dye, thereby reducing the fluorescence intensity (Scheme 1B). There­ fore, the PAT-induced door-locking strategy enabled the detection of PAT without the loss of sensitivity caused by competition-based transduction.
## 3.6. Optimization of experimental conditions
The release amount of dye from MOFs is an important indicator of the performance of fluorescence aptasensors. Hence, a series of con­ centrations and loading times of dyes were optimized. Different amounts of Rh 6G ( $\mathrm{0.108{-}1.08\ m M}$ ) were used for the assays. Fig. S9A presents the fluorescence corresponding to the released Rh 6G. The data were recorded over a period of 1 h. As expected, the fluorescence significantly increased when the Rh 6G concentration increased (to $0.864~\mathrm{mM}$ ). The fluorescence plateaued at higher concentrations. The optimal concen­ tration of Rh 6G was $0.864~\mathrm{mM}$ for achieving the maximum loading capacity. The adsorption time also played an important role in achieving the maximum loading capacity. The systems were incubated with Rh 6G for various times $(4{-}24\mathrm{h})$ . The trend presented in Fig. S9B is similar to the trend presented in Fig. S9A. The figures reveal the fluorescence in­ tensity of MOFs increased gradually from $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . Negligible changes at longer adsorption time were attributed to the adsorption equilibrium of the MOFs. Thus, $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ was chosen as the optimal adsorption time for the preparation of fluorescence aptasensors.
