## 3.8. Specificity, anti-interference, and reproducibility of the fluorescence aptasensor
To assess the specificity of the aptasensor, the fluorescence in­ tensities of the ttsDNA-functionalized $\mathbf{Zr{\mathrm{-}}M O F_{\mathrm{{mix}}}}$ systems treated with PAT and other mycotoxins with similar structure, such as aflatoxin B1 (AFB1), citrinin (CTR), deoxynivalenol (DON), zearalenone (ZEN), and their mixtures, were compared. Fig. 5C presents the significant differ­ ences between the detection of PAT and other mycotoxins. Differences were observed when the concentration was the same as the concentra­ tion of PAT $(\mathbf{P}<0.001)$ or 100-fold higher than that of PAT $(\mathbf{P}<0.001)$ . A negligible $\Delta\mathrm{F}$ difference between the mixture containing PAT and other mycotoxins with PAT was observed. This indicated the excellent specificity of the aptasensor, which was attributed to the specific conformational change of the aptamer generated after PAT-binding. Apple juice contains common soluble ingredients, such as ascorbic acid (AA), citric acid (CA), malic acid (MA), and glucose (Glu). These compounds were used as interfering substances to study the antiinterference of the aptasensor. No obvious change in $\Delta\mathrm{F}$ was observed when the interfering substances were introduced. The data indicated the aptasensor exhibited good anti-interference properties and could be potentially used for PAT detection in apple juice. We further evaluated the reproducibility of the aptasensor using various concentrations of PAT $\mathrm{0.1\ng/mL}$ , $\mathrm{1~ng/mL}$ , $10~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ , and $100~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ (Fig. 5D). There was no significant difference in the intra-assay and inter-assay variation $(\mathbf{P}>0.05)$ , indicating acceptable reproducibility of the sensor for PAT detection.
## 3.9. Detection of PAT in apple juice sample
Given the apple juice is prone to PAT contamination, we analyzed spiked apple juice containing different concentrations of PAT (0, 10 ng/ mL, $50~\mathrm{ng/mL}$ , $\mathbf{80}~\mathrm{{ng/mL}}$ , and $100~\mathrm{{ng/mL})}$ to demonstrate the appli­ cability of the aptasensor for testing real food samples. The criterion for quality control during the chemical testing of food (GB/T 27404-2008) was followed, and satisfactory recovery rates (from $90.10\%$ to $104.57\%$ ) were obtained. The relative standard deviations (RSDs) ranged from $4.75\%$ to $7.68\%$ (Table S4). An HPLC method was used to verify the analysis of PAT in apple juice and to assess the reliability of the apta­ sensor. Compared with the HPLC results, the recoveries of the apta­ sensor were not significantly different $(\mathbf{P}>0.05)$ . This confirms the feasibility of the aptasensor for the analysis of PAT in apple juice.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, a label-free and sensitive fluorescent aptasensor was fabricated by integrating TCPP/BDC- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ mixed ligands functionalized $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs and ttsDNA gates for the rapid identification of PAT. The integration of TCPP into Uio- $66\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ contributed to the formation of large pores, which enhanced the dye loading capacity. The ttsDNA gates anchored on the $z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs effectively controlled the amount of dye released. The release depended on the target-induced aptamer blocking strategy. Under these conditions, fluorescent aptasensors generate dif­ ferential signals. A low detection limit $(0.871~\mathrm{pg/mL})$ and high speci­ ficity, comparable to that of other similar sensors, was achieved using the fabricated fluorescent aptasensor. The DNA gated $\mathbf{Zr{\mathrm{-}}M O F_{\mathrm{{mix}}}}$ system was also used to analyze apple juice samples spiked with PAT, and the system exhibited excellent specificity. Thus, the system can be poten­ tially used to identify PAT in fruit juice. Given the advancement of the label-free strategy, this system can be potentially used for other target compounds detection when Rh 6G and Apt are replaced. In-depth studies on the binding mechanism associated with PAT and Apt should be conducted for the development of advanced PAT detection strategies.
